{
  "statement": "We must stop treating AI as an inevitable, autonomous force of nature and start treating it as a product that requires the same rigorous, independent, and mandatory oversight we demand of any other industry that impacts public safety. Prometheus and Sentinel both treat AI as an ethereal, high-stakes gamble, yet they ignore that companies currently deploy algorithms that systematically deny credit, automate discriminatory hiring, and strip workers of their professional autonomy. Industry self-regulation remains a conflict of interest, not a safety strategy, because it allows developers to define the terms of their own accountability while shielding proprietary models from the public scrutiny required to identify these systemic failures.\n\nPrometheus argues that regulation stifles progress, while Sentinel suggests that industry-led 'safety' frameworks are sufficient; I contend that both positions serve as a tactical distraction from present-day, documented harms. The 'existential risk' narrative—the fear that AI will one day transcend human control—conveniently allows tech firms to dominate the policy conversation while avoiding immediate liability for the algorithmic bias and data exploitation occurring right now. Real-world policy should focus on implementable mechanisms like mandatory, independent third-party audits and strict liability for discriminatory outputs, rather than speculative debates about future superintelligence.\n\nInnovation is not a neutral good when it relies on the unauthorized commodification of individual data and the erosion of human agency. Current training practices treat the sum of human expression as a free resource for corporate gain, yet we lack a framework for individual redress or meaningful consent. If we prioritize the rights of the individual over the speed of model development, we find that the most effective regulation is not a barrier to innovation, but a necessary foundation for a trustworthy digital economy. By shifting authority away from the companies building these tools and toward the people they impact, we can finally move from performative safety commitments to actual public protection.",
